{:edb/db-before-t 1326 :edb/db-after-t 1327 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534639 50 #inst "2026-10-12T05:37:17.363Z" 13194139534639 17592186045417 true] [17592186159770 1078 #edb/ref 17592186159773 13194139534639 17592186045417 true] [17592186161689 1071 "| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f_{Y|X}(y \\,|\\, 2)$ | $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ |\n\nFind the $\\textrm{E}\\big[Y \\,|\\, X=2\\big]$ given that the conditional probability mass function $f_{Y|X}(y \\,|\\, 2)$ is shown in the table above." 13194139534639 17592186045417 true] [17592186161689 1071 "| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\, \\mid \\, 2)$ | $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ |\n\nFind the $\\textrm{E}\\big\\begin{bmatrix}Y \\, & \\, X=2\\big\\end{bmatrix}$ given that the conditional probability mass function $f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\, \\mid \\, 2)$ is shown in the table above." 13194139534639 17592186045417 false] [17592186161689 1074 "The conditional expected value of  $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\big[Y \\,|\\, X=x\\big] = \\sum_{y} y \\, f_{Y | X}(y \\,|\\, x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=2] & ={\\sum }_{y}y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534639 17592186045417 true] [17592186161689 1074 "The conditional expected value of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\big\\begin{bmatrix}Y \\, & \\, X=x\\big\\end{bmatrix} = \\sum_{y} y \\, f_{Y \\mid X}(y \\, \\mid \\, x)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE\\begin{bmatrix}Y & X = 2\\end{bmatrix} &= ∑_(y)yf_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid 2) \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} + 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534639 17592186045417 false] [17592186161692 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{9}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\dfrac{8}{27}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{27}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\dfrac{2}{27}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ |\n\nFind $\\textrm{E}\\big[Y \\,|\\, X=2\\big]$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139534639 17592186045417 true] [17592186161692 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{9}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\dfrac{8}{27}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{27}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\dfrac{2}{27}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ |\n\nFind $\\textrm{E}\\big\\begin{bmatrix}Y \\, & \\, X=2\\big\\end{bmatrix}$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139534639 17592186045417 false] [17592186161692 1074 "Recall that the conditional expected value of  $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\big[Y \\,|\\, X=x\\big] = \\sum_{y} y \\, f_{Y | X}(y \\,|\\, x).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $f_{Y | X}(y \\,|\\, x),$ let's first find the marginal distribution for $X,$ corresponding to the row totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $f_X(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\dfrac{8}{27}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{27}$ | $\\dfrac{13}{27}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\dfrac{2}{27}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{27}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x$ is\n\n$$\nf_{Y | X}(y \\,|\\, x)  = \\dfrac{f(x,y)}{f_X(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding row total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x{:}$\n\n| $f_{Y | X}(y \\,|\\, x)$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ | $1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\dfrac{8}{13}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{13}$ | $1$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\dfrac{2}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{3}{5}$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=2] & ={\\sum }_{y}y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{8}{13}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534639 17592186045417 true] [17592186161692 1074 "Recall that the conditional expected value of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\big\\begin{bmatrix}Y \\, & \\, X=x\\big\\end{bmatrix} = \\sum_{y} y \\, f_{Y \\mid X}(y \\, \\mid \\, x)\n$$\n\nIn order to find $f_{Y \\mid X}(y \\, \\mid \\, x)$, let's first find the marginal distribution for $X$, corresponding to the row totals:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $f_X(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\dfrac{8}{27}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{27}$ | $\\dfrac{13}{27}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\dfrac{2}{27}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{27}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x$ is\n\n$$\nf_{Y \\mid X}(y \\, \\mid \\, x) = \\dfrac{f(x,y)}{f_X(x)}\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding row total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x{:}$\n\n| $f_{Y \\mid X}(y \\, \\mid \\, x)$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ | $1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\dfrac{8}{13}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{13}$ | $1$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\dfrac{2}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{3}{5}$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE\\begin{bmatrix}Y & X = 2\\end{bmatrix} &= ∑_(y)yf_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid 2) \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot \\frac{8}{13} + 2 \\cdot \\frac{5}{13} \\\\\n&= \\frac{18}{13}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534639 17592186045417 false] [17592186161699 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{9}$ |\n| $X=4$ | $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ |\n\nFind $\\textrm{E}\\big[X \\,|\\, Y=1\\big]$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139534639 17592186045417 true] [17592186161699 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=2$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{9}$ |\n| $X=4$ | $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ |\n\nFind $\\textrm{E}\\big\\begin{bmatrix}X \\, & \\, Y=1\\big\\end{bmatrix}$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139534639 17592186045417 false] [17592186161699 1074 "Recall that the conditional expected value of  $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\big[X \\,|\\, Y=y\\big] = \\sum_{x} x \\, f_{X | Y}(x \\,|\\, y).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $f_{X | Y}(x \\,|\\, y),$ let's first find the marginal distribution for $Y,$ corresponding to the column totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{9}$ |\n| $X=4$ | $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ |\n| $f_Y(y)$ | $\\dfrac{5}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{4}{9}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y$ is\n\n$$\nf_{X | Y}(x \\,|\\, y)  = \\dfrac{f(x,y)}{f_Y(y)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding column total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y{:}$\n\n| $f_{X | Y}(x \\,|\\, y)$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ |\n| $X=4$ | $\\dfrac{3}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=1] & ={\\sum }_{x}x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}+4\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}+6\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{5} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534639 17592186045417 true] [17592186161699 1074 "Recall that the conditional expected value of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\big\\begin{bmatrix}X \\, & \\, Y=y\\big\\end{bmatrix} = \\sum_{x} x \\, f_{X \\mid Y}(x \\, \\mid \\, y)\n$$\n\nIn order to find $f_{X \\mid Y}(x \\, \\mid \\, y)$, let's first find the marginal distribution for $Y$, corresponding to the column totals:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=2$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{9}$ |\n| $X=4$ | $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ |\n| $f_Y(y)$ | $\\dfrac{5}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{4}{9}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y$ is\n\n$$\nf_{X \\mid Y}(x \\, \\mid \\, y) = \\dfrac{f(x,y)}{f_Y(y)}\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding column total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y{:}$\n\n| $f_{X \\mid Y}(x \\, \\mid \\, y)$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=2$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ |\n| $X=4$ | $\\dfrac{3}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y = 1\\end{bmatrix} &= ∑_(x)xf_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid 1) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{5} + 4 \\cdot \\frac{3}{5} + 6 \\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{20}{5} \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534639 17592186045417 false] [17592186162123 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430542 13194139534639 17592186045417 true] [17592186162123 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430547 13194139534639 17592186045417 true] [17592186162123 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430549 13194139534639 17592186045417 true] [17592186162123 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430551 13194139534639 17592186045417 true] [17592186162123 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430553 13194139534639 17592186045417 true] [17592186162126 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430540 13194139534639 17592186045417 true] [17592186162126 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430541 13194139534639 17592186045417 true] [17592186162126 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430543 13194139534639 17592186045417 true] [17592186162126 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430550 13194139534639 17592186045417 true] [17592186162133 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430544 13194139534639 17592186045417 true] [17592186162133 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430545 1319413