obtain the conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x:$\n\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=2] & ={\\sum }_{y}y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2) \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534639 17592186045417 true] [17592186163289 1064 "For discrete random variables $X$  and  $Y$, the **conditional expected value of $X$ given $Y=y$** is defined by\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X|Y=y]=\\sum_{x}x f_{X|Y}(x|y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f_{X|Y}(x|y)$ is the conditional probability mass function of $X$ given $Y.$ \n\nThis definition is similar to that of $\\textrm{E}[X].$ However, we now use the conditional PMF $f_{X|Y}(x|y)$ instead of the marginal PMF $f_X(x)$ in the summation.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The conditional expectation $\\textrm E[X | Y = y]$ is (in general) a function of $y.$ This makes intuitive sense. If the distribution of $X$ varies with $Y,$ then the expected value of $X$ for a given $Y$ must vary with the value of $Y.$\n\n- If $y$ is replaced with a number, then $\\textrm E[X | Y = y]$ returns a single value.\n\n- If $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then $\\textrm E[X | Y = y] = \\textrm E[X]$ for all $y$. Again, this makes intuitive sense. If the outcome of $Y$ has no influence on the outcome of $X,$ then the distribution of $X$ and, consequently, its mean, is also unaffected by $Y.$\n\n- We sometimes use the notation $\\mu_{X|y}$ to denote $E[X | Y = y].$\n\nFinally, we have an analogous definition for $\\textrm E[Y|X=x]\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[Y|X=x]=\\sum_{y}y f_{Y|X}(y|x)\n$$" 13194139534639 17592186045417 true] [17592186163289 1064 "For discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$, the **conditional expected value of $X$ given $Y=y$** is defined by\n\n$$\n\\textrm E\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y=y\\end{bmatrix}=\\sum_{x}x f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid y)$ is the conditional probability mass function of $X$ given $Y$.\n\nThis definition is similar to that of $\\textrm{E}[X]$. However, we now use the conditional PMF $f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid y)$ instead of the marginal PMF $f_X(x)$ in the summation.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The conditional expectation $\\textrm E\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y = y\\end{bmatrix}$ is (in general) a function of $y$. This makes intuitive sense. If the distribution of $X$ varies with $Y$, then the expected value of $X$ for a given $Y$ must vary with the value of $Y$.\n- If $y$ is replaced with a number, then $\\textrm E\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y = y\\end{bmatrix}$ returns a single value.\n- If $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then $\\textrm E\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y = y\\end{bmatrix} = \\textrm E[X]$ for all $y$. Again, this makes intuitive sense. If the outcome of $Y$ has no influence on the outcome of $X$, then the distribution of $X$ and, consequently, its mean, is also unaffected by $Y$.\n- We sometimes use the notation $\\mu_{X \\mid y}$ to denote $E\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y = y\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFinally, we have an analogous definition for\n\n$$\n\\textrm E\\begin{bmatrix}Y & X=x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\textrm E\\begin{bmatrix}Y & X=x\\end{bmatrix}=\\sum_{y}y f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid x)\n$$" 13194139534639 17592186045417 false] [17592186430456 1080 "4.9" 13194139534639 17592186045417 true] [17592186430456 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534639 17592186045417 true] [17592186430456 1083 #uuid "012ca9e0-686a-5d19-b689-9cff442f5919" 13194139534639 17592186045417 true] [17592186430457 1080 "2.4" 13194139534639 17592186045417 true] [17592186430457 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534639 17592186045417 true] [17592186430457 1083 #uuid "04d3bd28-c2f2-5491-924f-6e0c82e09fc1" 13194139534639 17592186045417 true] [17592186430458 1080 "3.05" 13194139534639 17592186045417 t